description:
## Caller contract risk: a wire-invalid \`parentId\` deserializes silently and round-trips back.

RFC 8621 §2.3 defines \`Mailbox/query\` \`parentId\` filter as a three-way value: absent, JSON \`null\`, or a String matching a Mailbox \`Id\`. The spec admits no other shape.

The implemented type is \`Option<serde_json::Value>\` with a custom deserializer (\`deserialize_parent_id_three_way\`) that just calls \`serde_json::Value::deserialize\` and wraps the result in \`Some\`. That call accepts ANY JSON value: numbers, booleans, arrays, nested objects, all silently succeed. The serializer then echoes back whatever was deserialized.

### Concrete failure modes

Today's type permits all of these to round-trip:

```rust
// Spec-valid:
let _: MailboxFilterCondition = serde_json::from_value(json!({\"parentId\": null}))?;
let _: MailboxFilterCondition = serde_json::from_value(json!({\"parentId\": \"m1\"}))?;

// Spec-INVALID but accepted today:
let _: MailboxFilterCondition = serde_json::from_value(json!({\"parentId\": 42}))?;
let _: MailboxFilterCondition = serde_json::from_value(json!({\"parentId\": true}))?;
let _: MailboxFilterCondition = serde_json::from_value(json!({\"parentId\": [\"m1\"]}))?;
let _: MailboxFilterCondition = serde_json::from_value(json!({\"parentId\": {\"id\": \"m1\"}}))?;
```

A client that receives a malformed \`parentId: 42\` from a non-conformant server has no signal that anything is wrong. A client that constructs \`parent_id = Some(Value::Bool(true))\` programmatically can send wire-invalid filters that the server will reject at the JSON-parsing layer (if it is conformant) or silently misinterpret (if it is not).

### Why the chosen shape is type-untyped

The doc comment on the field describes three valid states, but \`Option<serde_json::Value>\` is the universal sum of \"absent OR any JSON value\". The type system carries no information beyond what's in the doc.

A type-safe shape exists:

```rust
#[non_exhaustive]
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ParentIdFilter {
    /// Match only top-level mailboxes (wire: \`null\`).
    TopLevelOnly,
    /// Match mailboxes whose parent equals this Id (wire: the Id string).
    Equals(Id),
}

pub parent_id: Option<ParentIdFilter>,
```

With a manual \`Serialize\` / \`Deserialize\` impl (\`null\` <-> \`TopLevelOnly\`, string <-> \`Equals(Id::from(s))\`, anything else <-> error). Wire-compatible with the existing format; callers cannot construct or accept wire-invalid values.

### Test coverage

The added tests (\`mailbox_filter_parent_id_absent_round_trips\`, \`_null_round_trips\`, \`_string_round_trips\`) cover the three spec-valid cases but do NOT include a negative test asserting that a non-spec value (e.g. \`{\"parentId\": 42}\`) is rejected. The implementation today silently accepts it.

### Scope note

This is in the diff: the custom deserializer was added in this change. Before this diff, the field was \`Option<serde_json::Value>\` with the default \`Option<T>\` Deserialize impl (which collapses \`null\` to \`None\` — the bug the diff was fixing). The fix introduced the three-way distinction but did not narrow the value space; both the pre- and post-diff shapes accept non-spec values, but the doc comment is now more explicit about what \"valid\" means, which deepens the contract-vs-type mismatch.

### Cross-references

- Workspace AGENTS.md Filter-algebra-exclusion clause: filter conditions are correctness-sensitive; silent acceptance of unknown clauses is a query-correctness hazard. Silently accepting a non-spec \`parentId\` shape is in the same family.
